From: TekRantGaming <craigferels896@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 17:20:55 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 11/12] Linux: free the guest memory however the process ends

The guest address space is a POSIX shared memory object (/dev/shm, RAM).
Its name was only removed on a clean shutdown, so a crash or a kill left
the whole thing in /dev/shm until a reboot, and once that filled up the
next run died with SIGBUS while mapping it. Only the descriptor is used
after creation, so the name is now removed right away.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/core/memory_posix.cpp | 5 +++++
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/core/memory_posix.cpp b/src/core/memory_posix.cpp
index b52bf39..00e43c5 100644
--- a/src/core/memory_posix.cpp
+++ b/src/core/memory_posix.cpp
@@ -483,6 +483,11 @@ FileMappingHandle CreateFileMappingHandle(const std::filesystem::path& path, siz
     shm_unlink(full_path.c_str());
     return kFileMappingHandleInvalid;
   }
+  // Only the descriptor is used from here on, so remove the name now: the
+  // memory then goes away with the process however it ends. Otherwise a
+  // crash or a kill leaves the whole guest address space in /dev/shm (RAM)
+  // until a reboot, and once that fills the next run dies with SIGBUS.
+  shm_unlink(full_path.c_str());
   return static_cast<FileMappingHandle>(ret);
 #endif
 }
